# INC-001: HSRP Core Switch Failover Test

**Status:** Resolved — redundancy confirmed working as designed

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | Sev3 — planned failover validation test, redundant path present by design |
| **Time Detected** | [fill in] |
| **Symptoms** | Brief, expected interruption to Sales and IT VLAN traffic during the Core A outage; Finance (already Active on Core B) unaffected |
| **Diagnostic Steps** | 1. Attempted `int range fa0/1-fa0/3` on Core A — rejected (`Invalid input`); Packet Tracer requires comma-separated interfaces rather than a dash range in this context. Used `int range fa0/1,fa0/2,fa0/3` instead.<br>2. Shut down those three interfaces — Core A's links to Core B (Fa0/1), Edge Router (Fa0/2), and the access-layer trunk (Fa0/3). Core A's dedicated Monitoring Server port was intentionally left untouched, so this simulates loss of Core A's upstream/downstream reachability rather than a full power failure.<br>3. Started a continuous ping from an IT VLAN host (PC3) to a Sales VLAN host (10.10.10.6) to observe real packet loss during the transition.<br>4. Confirmed on Core A's own log: OSPF adjacencies to Core B (10.255.255.5) and Edge Router (203.0.113.1) dropped `FULL to DOWN`; Vlan10 and Vlan30 SVIs went line-protocol-down (Vlan20 stayed up locally via the untouched Monitoring Server port).<br>5. Confirmed on Core B: OSPF adjacency to Core A (10.255.255.1) dropped, immediately followed by `HSRP-6-STATECHANGE: Vlan10 Grp 10 state Standby -> Active` and the same for Vlan20. `show standby br` confirmed Core B Active on all three VIPs, with Core A's status showing as "unknown" (adjacency lost). |
| **Root Cause** | N/A — deliberate fault injection to validate HSRP redundancy, not a misconfiguration |
| **Resolution** | Re-enabled Core A's three interfaces (`no shutdown`). OSPF adjacency with Core B recovered (`LOADING to FULL`). Because `preempt` is configured on Core A for Sales/IT, Core B's log showed `Vlan10/Vlan20 Grp state Speak -> Standby` as Core A reclaimed Active automatically. Final `show standby br` on Core B confirmed Core A Active again for Sales/IT, Core B correctly back to Standby for those two, Finance undisturbed throughout. |
| **Time to Recover** | 4 consecutive dropped pings (~4 seconds at default 1/sec interval) before replies resumed — well within HSRP's default 10-second hold timer. |
| **Prevention / Recommendation** | Default HSRP timers produced acceptable failover for ICMP; for more latency-sensitive traffic, consider tuning hello/hold timers lower. Note: a genuinely clean "every interface down" test (rather than the 3 specific uplinks used here) would more fully simulate total device loss — worth repeating as a follow-up test if time allows. |

## Known Platform Quirk (observed, not a config bug)

Core A's own log showed `HSRP-6-STATECHANGE: Vlan30 Grp 30 state Standby -> Active` immediately after Vlan30's line protocol went down on that same device — logically inconsistent, since a switch shouldn't become HSRP Active on an interface that just failed. This is very likely a Packet Tracer HSRP simulation inaccuracy rather than a real design issue; included here for documentation honesty rather than presented as a clean, fully-explained result.

